chapter2: defining non functional requirements

performance metrics:
1. response time => 
    the elapsed time from the moment when a user makes a request until they receive the request answer
    in terms of performance metrics, user cares about the most
2. throughtput => 
    the number of requests or the data volumne per second that the system is processing
    determines the required computing resources (e.g. how many servers you need)
    a system is scalable if its max throughtput can be significantly increased by adding compuring resources

linear scalability:
1. if doubling the resources will enable you to handle twice the load while keeping performance the same
2. this is considered a good thing

scaling types:
1. vertical scaling or scaling up => move to a more powerful machine
2. shared memory architecture => getting parallelism on a machine by sharing multiple processes or threads
3. shared disk architecture => several machines but stores data on an array of disks that is shared
4. shared nothing architecture or horizontal scaling or scaling out => distributed systems with multiple nodes

complexity:
1. accidental => arises only because of limitation of tooling
2. essential => inherent in the problem domain
-. this distinction is flawed, because boundaries between the essential and the accidental shift as tooling evolves

